{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786514934,"nonce":"b+kklrzM/ioy8euthuYailV162U=","title":"**What is wrong.** The closed set of timeline event types is written out twice, by hand, in two languages, and nothing compares the two lists. `alertdomain.AllEventTypes()` (`internal/alerts/domain/event.go:130-147`) returns 36 values; `AlertEventType` in `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:5196-5234` enumerates 36 strings. They agree today, value for value. That agreement is maintained by whoever edits both files remembering to edit both files.","message":"**The demonstration.** `AllEventTypes` appears in five files in the tree: its definition and `init` (`internal/alerts/domain/event.go:123,130`), its own unit test (`internal/alerts/domain/event_test.go:16-64`), a comment in `internal/alerts/api/dto.go:481`, and one caller in a contract test (`internal/grouping/api/groups_contract_test.go:922`). None of them opens the contract. The Go files that do open the contract are all under `test/contract/` — `schema/schema.go`, `dto_schema_test.go`, `openapi_doc_test.go`, `coverage_test.go`, `tenancy_test.go`, `server/conformance_test.go` — and `dto_schema_test.go` imports thirteen `internal/*/api` packages (`:18-30`) and no `domain` package at all. No test in the repository has both the contract enum and `AllEventTypes()` in scope, so none can be comparing them.\n\nThe DDL is not a second copy either, despite `event.go:128-129` saying the list is what \"the timeline renderer and the DDL CHECK are both generated from\": `ev_type_ck` is `CHECK (type ~ '^[a-z_]+\\.[a-z_]+$')` (`db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:245`), a SHAPE, not a value list. It admits any `\u003csubject\u003e.\u003cfact\u003e` string. The contract enum is the only enumeration of these 36 values outside Go.\n\nThe concrete failure. A 37th event type is added to `event.go`, and because the `?type=` ceiling is the size of the enum, both `validate:\"omitempty,max=36,unique\"` tags are bumped to 37 — `internal/alerts/api/dto.go:485` and `internal/grouping/api/dto.go:268`. Both existing gates stay green:\n\n`TestEnumFilterCeilingsMatchTheirEnum` (`test/contract/dto_schema_test.go:1066-1106`) compares the contract's `maxItems` to the contract's OWN enum. Both are still 36, so equality holds and the gate passes while the ceiling is now below the set the server writes.\n\n`TestTheTimelineAcceptsEveryDeclaredEventType` (`internal/grouping/api/groups_contract_test.go:917-936`) asks for all 37 names and expects 200. It gets one: `NewEventType` (`event.go:150-156`) parses against the same domain map the new value was added to, and `max=37` now admits the count. The only contract assertion the test then makes is `schema.Assert(t, \"getAlertGroupTimeline\", http.StatusOK, resp.Body())`, and `schema.Assert` validates the RESPONSE body against the contract (`test/contract/schema/schema.go:369-383`); nothing validates a request's query parameters against the contract's parameter schema, and `test/contract/apitest/apitest.go` contains no such check. So that test's own claim — *\"This asks for the whole of `alertdomain.AllEventTypes()` by name, so the 37th event type fails here rather than in a client\"* (`:915-916`) — does not hold. It would pass.\n\nWhat breaks instead is downstream, and only there. Every other link in the chain is gated: contract → generated client is gate G3 (`npm run generate:check`, `web/package.json:16`, run by `justfile:274` and `.github/workflows/ci.yml:154`), and generated client → UI is TypeScript itself, because `EVENT_KINDS` is declared `Record\u003cAlertEventType, EventKind\u003e` (`web/src/features/alerts/detail/eventKinds.ts:52`) and would not compile with a member missing. Both hold — and both stay quiet, because the contract never changed. The 37th type reaches the timeline as a value no contract-generated artefact knows: `web/src/api/generated/validators.ts:99` is a valibot picklist of exactly those 36 strings and `web/src/api/generated/schema.d.ts:2236` the matching union, so any consumer that validates responses against the published contract rejects a timeline page containing it, and no client can name it in a `?type=` filter at all. oto's own UI degrades rather than rejects — `web/src/api/client.ts:289-302` says plainly that response bodies are not parsed yet — so `kindOf` falls through to `UNKNOWN_KIND` (`eventKinds.ts:296-297`) and the new fact renders as a grey dot labelled \"Event\".\n\n**Why it matters.** The first link in that chain is the only unguarded one, and it is the link where the value is actually born. The timeline is the view that IS the product, and the type is what tells a reader whether they are looking at a re-fire, an expiry or a rule change; an event that arrives as \"Event\" on a grey dot has been added to the history and subtracted from the story. `NewEventType`'s own error text says *\"adding one requires a SPEC amendment\"* (`event.go:153`) — the process is already understood to be a two-file edit, which is exactly the kind of process a gate should be enforcing rather than trusting.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/domain/event.go:130-147` (the 36) and `:128-129` (the comment that overstates what the DDL holds); `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:5196-5234` (the other 36); `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:245` (`ev_type_ck`, shape only); `test/contract/dto_schema_test.go:1066-1106` and `:18-30` (the gate that cannot see the domain); `internal/grouping/api/groups_contract_test.go:900-936` (the test whose comment claims the coverage); `test/contract/schema/schema.go:369-383` (response-only assertion); `internal/alerts/api/dto.go:485` and `internal/grouping/api/dto.go:268` (the two `max=36` tags).\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** One test in `test/contract/` reads `components.schemas.AlertEventType` and `alertdomain.AllEventTypes()` and fails unless they are the same set in the same order, naming the values present on one side and absent from the other. The comment at `internal/grouping/api/groups_contract_test.go:915-916` either becomes true or stops claiming the coverage, and `internal/alerts/domain/event.go:128-129` stops saying the DDL CHECK is generated from the list when it is a shape regex. The 37th event type then fails in CI, in the repository that owns both copies, rather than in somebody's generated client.","files":null}]}